            <h2>How to prepare for a podcast recording session</h2>
            <p>Follow these 6 steps before your recording day:</p>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 mb-4 space-y-1 text-[1rem] leading-[1.85] text-[rgba(15,14,14,0.75)]">
              <li><strong>Write a complete script or outline</strong> — You don't need to memorize word-for-word, but know what each segment covers and in what order.</li>
              <li><strong>Do at least one practice run-through</strong> — Say the actual content out loud before recording day. You'll find where you stumble and which sections run long.</li>
              <li><strong>Tell the studio how many people are recording</strong> — For interviews or panels, let them know the headcount so the right number of microphones are set up before you arrive.</li>
              <li><strong>Send topic points to your guest in advance</strong> — Guests who know the topics beforehand speak more fluently and need fewer pauses to think during the session.</li>
              <li><strong>Stay well-hydrated before recording</strong> — Good vocal quality comes from a hydrated throat. Limit coffee before the session.</li>
              <li><strong>Set a target episode length</strong> — If you want a 30-minute episode, plan to record 35-40 minutes to allow for editing.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>For a more detailed recording preparation guide, read <a href={`/${lang}/blog/recording-studio-guide`}>How to prepare for your first studio session</a>.</p>

            <h2>Podcast recording formats</h2>
            <p>Podcasts come in several formats, each requiring a different setup:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Solo host</strong> — One person speaking. Needs one microphone. The simplest setup to arrange — focus goes entirely on script and delivery.</li>
              <li><strong>Two-person interview</strong> — Host and guest in the same studio. Needs two microphones and a mixer with at least two input channels.</li>
              <li><strong>Panel discussion (3+ people)</strong> — Host plus multiple guests. Requires a multi-mic setup and sufficient mixer channels for everyone.</li>
              <li><strong>Remote guest</strong> — Guest in a different location, recorded via video call. Requires the guest to also record their own audio locally (double-ender technique), then the two tracks are combined in post.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Thunder Studio's Control Room supports solo, interview, and panel formats. See setup details on the <a href={`/${lang}/rooms/control-room`}>Control Room page</a>.</p>

